 After reflecting on it, I found the key lies in observing patterns in the terms of the product. Notice that
\begin{align*}
f(k^2) &= k^4 + k^2 + 1 \\
&= (k^4 + 2k^2 + 1) - k^2 \\
&= (k^2 + 1)^2 - k^2 \\
&= (k^2 + k + 1)(k^2 - k + 1) \\
&= f(k)f(k-1),
\end{align*}
for $k > 1.$ For $k = 1,$ we have $f(1) = 3.$ Therefore,
\begin{align*}
1000 f(1^2) f(2^2) \dotsm f(n^2) &\ge f(1)^2 f(2)^2 ... f(n)^2 \\
\Rightarrow 1000 (f(2)f(1)^2)(f(3)f(2)^2)\dotsm (f(n)f(n-1)^2) &\ge f(1)^2 f(2)^2 ... f(n)^2 \\
\Rightarrow f(n) &\ge 1000. \end{align*}
Since $f(14) = 1981 < 10000$ and  $f(15) = 2278 > 10000,$ we have $n = \boxed{14}.$ <answer> 14 </answer>

